{"conditionalSetIds":[],"forecasterId":"demographics-ai-2036:run2:inflation:historian","informationSetArtifactId":"sha256:e096fb577bb0d8324b9bfe9d6066503d80fb6560fcca1e31753f09c698874d2c","modelForecastIds":[],"modelUse":"none","prediction":{"kind":"quantiles","quantiles":[{"probability":0.1,"value":0.7},{"probability":0.5,"value":2.15},{"probability":0.9,"value":3.6}]},"questionHash":"sha256:20d8e05d3bea415eb4452ff8b599c74a3c3ce1a1b0c2ce11dda57a4e19490197","questionId":"demographics-ai-2036-cq4","questionVersion":"1.0.0","rationale":"STARTING LEVEL, computed from the locked prior. 2A gives US 10y TIPS ~2.60% (FRED DFII10, 30 Oct 2026). G7 nominal GDP weights (US ~$30T of ~$50.5T = 59%; Japan 8.3%; Germany 9.3%; UK 7.3%; France 6.3%; Italy 4.8%; Canada 4.6%) applied to current real 10y yields (US 2.60, Germany ~0.6, UK ~1.5, France ~1.2, Italy ~2.2, Japan ~0.2, Canada ~1.8) gives a GDP-weighted start of ~1.98%, i.e. ~2.0%. Note the measure is US-dominated, so this is 60% a US TIPS question.\nREFERENCE CLASS: G7 GDP-weighted 10y real yields by decade (reconstructed; my search did not return a confirmed decade-average series - the Bursa.ro G7 linker chart and the Jorda-Knoll-Kuvshinov-Schularick-Taylor long-run dataset are the sources that would settle it, so treat these as approximate): 1970s ~-1.5% (deeply negative ex post); 1980s ~+4.5%; 1990s ~+4.0%; 2000s ~+2.2%; 2010s ~+0.3%; 2020-2025 ~+0.3% average, ending ~+2.0%. Long-run 1960-2025 mean ~+2.0-2.5%.\nBASE RATE OF STAYING NEAR THE STARTING LEVEL: low. Of the 5 decade-to-decade transitions since 1970, only 1980s->1990s stayed within ~0.5pp; the other 4 moved >=1.5pp. So P(a decade average within +/-0.5pp of its start) ~ 20%. This forces a WIDE distribution, which is the main thing my lens contributes here.\nP50 = 2.15%. Two reasons to sit essentially at the starting level despite the low \"stays put\" base rate: (i) 2.0-2.2% IS the long-run 1960-2025 mean, so the starting point and the mean-reversion target coincide - the one configuration in which a flat central forecast is the base-rate answer rather than a lazy one; (ii) the locked 2A arithmetic is directionally upward (debt >100% of GDP by 2029, ~142% US and ~127% China by 2031, net interest 2%->3% of GDP, 3-yr P95 Debt-at-Risk ~117% and +2.4pp under a 20% AI-led equity shock, eroding convenience yields, levered basis-trade absorption) while the IMF's own aging result is that (r-g) worsens ~+1.0pp mainly through g falling ~1.1pp \"even where a frictionless r* eases\". Those roughly offset in the level and both widen the tails.\nP90 = 3.60%: the 1980s/1990s term-premium regime, reachable from a 2% start by a 1.5pp move - smaller than 4 of the 5 historical decade transitions. P10 = 0.70%: the financial-repression / post-bust / flight-to-quality regime, which has a 2-in-6 decade base rate (1970s negative, 2010s ~0.3%) and is the regime a capex bust plus the BIS Box C demand deficit would produce. Mildly right-skewed because the debt arithmetic is one-directional and the zero lower bound is no longer the binding floor it was in the 2010s.","referenceClassIds":[],"schemaVersion":"forecast-workbench.forecast/v1","sealedAt":"2026-11-04T17:31:56.351Z","seriesId":"published-panel","strongestContraryCase":"Not elicited per question. Panelist falsification signposts: {\"signpost\":\"OECD-38 GDP-weighted core CPI/PCE (OECD MEI) 12-month average >=3.0% for calendar year 2029, together with US and euro-area core SERVICES EX-SHELTER >=3.5% for the same year. The ex-shelter cut is the test that isolates my basket-weight argument from a rent-driven print.\",\"by_when\":\"Jan 31, 2030 (full-year 2029 data)\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Falsifies the claim that OECD core is capped at 2.1-2.4% because the hot items sit outside core. CQ2 rises from 14 to ~25; CQ6-C rises ~+8 to ~21 and CQ6-D falls ~-8 to ~9; CQ4 P50 rises ~+0.3pp to ~2.45%; hypothesis plank (c) falls from 58 to ~35.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"China headline CPI (NBS) 12-month average >=2.0% for calendar year 2029, with household final consumption expenditure >=45% of GDP in the same year. Both conditions required, so a food/pork spike alone does not trigger it.\",\"by_when\":\"Feb 28, 2030 (NBS annual release)\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Falsifies the Japan-analogue reference class that carries conjunct B of my CQ2. CQ2 falls from 14 to ~7; CQ3 P50 rises ~+0.2pp to ~2.2% and CQ3 P10 rises to ~1.3%; CQ6-A rises ~+4, CQ6-D falls ~-5.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"OECD-38 harmonized unemployment stays <=5.0% as an annual average every year 2027-2031 with no QUARTER above 5.5%, AND OECD hourly earnings in ISIC F+Q outpace J+K+M by >=4.0pp cumulative over 2025-2031 (half the CQ1 bar, arriving on schedule).\",\"by_when\":\"Mar 31, 2032\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Both CQ1 conjuncts would be tracking, against a 31% unconditional base rate on the first and ~17% on the second. CQ1 rises from 11 to ~25; CQ6-B rises ~+5 at the expense of CQ6-D; hypothesis plank (a) rises from 4 to ~12 (the barbell would be real even if reallocation is not smooth).\"} | {\"signpost\":\"IEA or EIA strict OPERATIONAL data-centre electricity consumption for OECD-38 + China >=650 TWh for calendar year 2030, with no cumulative tally of announced-hyperscaler-capex cancellations or impairments reaching 20%.\",\"by_when\":\"Jul 31, 2031 (IEA Electricity / World Energy Investment 2031)\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Falsifies my downward revision of AI-Q3 and, more importantly, my 2031-2036 capex-digestion disinflation. CQ3 P50 rises ~+0.3pp to ~2.3% and P90 to ~3.3%; CQ6-A rises from 17 to ~25 and CQ6-D falls to ~10; CQ4 P50 rises ~+0.25pp on the real-rate/investment-demand channel; plank (d) rises from 13 to ~25.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"A MSCI World peak-to-trough drawdown >=25% occurs before Dec 31, 2030 AND G7 GDP-weighted 10y NOMINAL sovereign yields fall >=100bp during that drawdown window.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2030\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"The flight-to-quality regime still govern